NNAAMMEE
       ppmtoarbtxt - generate image in arbitrary text format from PPM image


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppppmmttooaarrbbttxxtt _b_o_d_y_t_m_p_l [--hhdd _h_e_a_d_t_m_p_l] [--ttll _t_a_i_l_t_m_p_l] [_p_p_m_f_i_l_e]


DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppppmmttooaarrbbttxxtt  generates simple text-based graphics formats based on for‐
       mat descriptions given as input.  A text-based graphics format  is  one
       in  which  an  image is represented by text (like PNM plain format, but
       unlike PNM raw format).

       ppppmmttooaarrbbttxxtt reads a PPM image as input.  For each pixel in  the  image,
       ppppmmttooaarrbbttxxtt  writes  the  contents  of the template file _b_o_d_y_t_m_p_l, with
       certain substitutions based on the value of the pixel, to Standard Out‐
       put.

       You  may  also  supply  a head template file, in which case ppppmmttooaarrbbttxxtt
       generates text from the template file, based on the  image  dimensions,
       and includes it in the output before anything else.

       Likewise,  you  may  supply  a  tail  template file to cause text to be
       placed at the end of the output.



   TTeemmppllaattee FFiilleess
       The text that ppppmmttooaarrbbttxxtt generates from a template file is the literal
       text  of the template file, except with substitution specifier replaced
       with something else.  The program recognizes a  substitution  specifier
       as text of the form ##((...)).

       ppppmmttooaarrbbttxxtt  treats  white  space in the template files the same as any
       other characters, placing it in the output, with one exception: If  the
       template  file ends with a newline character, ppppmmttooaarrbbttxxtt ignores it --
       it does not include it in the output.

       Many substitution specifiers use format strings (another form  of  tem‐
       plate)  to  specify  the  substitution.   You  should make these format
       strings as minimal as possible, placing literal text outside  the  sub‐
       stitution specifier instead of inside the format string.  For example,

       Wrong: #(flum %%%2.2f 0 1)

       Right: %#(flum %2.2f 0 1)

       The  valid  substitution  specifiers are as follows.  Text that has the
       form of a substitution  specifier  but  is  not  actually  valid  (e.g.
       ##((rraannddoomm  jjuunnkk))  usually just specifies its literal value, but if it is
       close enough to something valid, ppppmmttooaarrbbttxxtt assumes you made a mistake
       and fails.

       Useful  in  a  body template, to do substitutions based on a particular
       pixel:



       ##((iirreedd _f_o_r_m_a_t _b_l_a_c_k_r_e_f _w_h_i_t_e_r_e_f))
              generates an integer in the range _b_l_a_c_k_r_e_f to _w_h_i_t_e_r_e_f in a for‐
              mat  specified  by  _f_o_r_m_a_t representing the red intensity of the
              pixel.  A red intensity of 0 becomes _b_l_a_c_k_r_e_f; a  red  intensity
              of  maxval becomes _w_h_i_t_e_r_e_f, with the rest linearly interpolated
              in between.

              _f_o_r_m_a_t is a printf-like format specifier like "%d".  ppppmmttooaarrbbttxxtt
              uses as the entire format string to a ffpprriinnttff POSIX library call
              whose only other argument is the red itensity as an integer data
              type.   ppppmmttooaarrbbttxxtt does not necessarily verify that your format
              string makes sense; there are  values  you  could  specify  that
              could  even  crash  the  program.  To avoid unexpected behavior,
              keep format strings simple and hardcoded, and  never  include  a
              per cent sign or newline.

              ##((iirreedd)) is equivalent to ##((iirreedd %%dd 00 225555)).


       ##((iiggrreeeenn _f_o_r_m_a_t _b_l_a_c_k_r_e_f _w_h_i_t_e_r_e_f))
              Same as ##((iirreedd......, but for green.


       ##((iibblluuee _f_o_r_m_a_t _b_l_a_c_k_r_e_f _w_h_i_t_e_r_e_f))
              Same as ##((iirreedd......, but for blue.


       ##((iilluumm _f_o_r_m_a_t _b_l_a_c_k_r_e_f _w_h_i_t_e_r_e_f))
              Same   as   ##((iirreedd......,  but  representing  the  luminance  value
              (0.299*red + 0.587*green + 0.114*blue) of the pixel.


       ##((ffrreedd _f_o_r_m_a_t _b_l_a_c_k_r_e_f _w_h_i_t_e_r_e_f))
              Same as ##((iirreedd......, but generates a floating point number instead
              of an integer.

              In  this case, the second argument to the ffpprriinnttff that uses _f_o_r_‐
              _m_a_t has a double precision floating point data type.

              ##((ffrreedd)) is equivalent to ##((ffrreedd %%ff 00..00 11..00)).


       ##((ffggrreeeenn _f_o_r_m_a_t _b_l_a_c_k_r_e_f _w_h_i_t_e_r_e_f))
              Same as ##((ffrreedd......, but for green.


       ##((ffbblluuee _f_o_r_m_a_t _b_l_a_c_k_r_e_f _w_h_i_t_e_r_e_f))
              Same as ##((ffrreedd......, but for blue.


       ##((fflluumm _f_o_r_m_a_t _b_l_a_c_k_r_e_f _w_h_i_t_e_r_e_f))
              Same  as  ##((ffrreedd......,  but  representing  the   luminance   value
              (0.299*red + 0.587*green + 0.114*blue) of the pixel.


       ##((ppoossxx _f_o_r_m_a_t))
              Generates  the  horizontal position of the pixel, in pixels from
              the left edge of the image.

              The second argument to the ffpprriinnttff that uses _f_o_r_m_a_t has  an  un‐
              signed integer data type.

              _f_o_r_m_a_t defaults to %%uu


       ##((ppoossyy _f_o_r_m_a_t))
              Same as ##((wwiiddtthh......, but for the vertical position.



       If  you  use any of the above substitution specifiers in a head or tail
       template, the result is undefined.

       Useful in a head or tail template, to do substitutions based on  whole-
       image attributes:



       ##((wwiiddtthh _f_o_r_m_a_t))
              Generates the width in pixels of the image.

              The  second  argument to the ffpprriinnttff that uses _f_o_r_m_a_t has an un‐
              signed integer data type.

              _f_o_r_m_a_t defaults to %%uu


       ##((hheeiigghhtt _f_o_r_m_a_t))
              Same as ##((wwiiddtthh......, but for the height of the image.





OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common  Options  ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩  ), ppppmmttooaarrbbttxxtt recognizes
       the following command line options:



       --hhdd _h_e_a_d_t_m_p_l
              This option specifies a head template (_h_e_a_d_t_m_p_l is the  name  of
              the head template file); it causes ppppmmttooaarrbbttxxtt to place the con‐
              tents of the file named _h_e_a_d_t_m_p_l at the beginning of the output


       --ttll _t_a_i_l_t_m_p_l
              This option specifies a tail template; it is analogous to --hhdd.




EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
   ggrraayy iinnvveerrssiioonn
       Here we generate a PGM plain-format image  with  gray  inversion  (like
       ppppmmttooppggmm || ppnnmmiinnvveerrtt).

       Contents of our head template file:

       P2
       #(width) #(height)
       255


       Contents of our body skeleton file:

       #(ilum %d 255 0)



   ppoovvrraayy ffiillee
       Here  we  generate  a  povray file where each pixel is represented by a
       sphere at location (x,y,z) = (posx,height-posy,luminance).   The  color
       of the sphere is the color of the pixel.

       Contents of our head skeleton:

       #include "colors.inc"
       #include "textures.inc"
       camera {
          location  <#(width) * 0.6, #(height) * 0.7, 80>
          look_at   <#(width) * 0.5, #(height) * 0.5, 0>
       }

       light_source { <#(width) * 0.5, #(height) * 0.5, 25> color White
       }


       Contents of our body skeleton:

       sphere { <#(posx),#(height)-#(posy),#(ilum %d 0 10)>, 0.5
         texture {
           pigment {
             color rgb <#(fred),#(fgreen),#(fblue)>
           }
           finish {
             phong 1
           }
         }
       }



